          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I stopped my blood pressure tablets two months ago and my home readings are now around 155/95. Is that an emergency, and what does guidance describe about treatment and follow-up?",
          "answer": "You should seek medical advice regarding your blood pressure readings around 155/95 mm Hg. Although this does not indicate an emergency, it is essential to monitor your condition closely and consult a healthcare provider, especially since you have stopped taking your medication.\n\nCurrent guidelines suggest that for elevated blood pressure like yours, treatment typically begins with lifestyle changes. If you have risk factors such as cardiovascular disease or diabetes, medication should be initiated alongside lifestyle adjustments. First-line medications often include thiazide-type diuretics, ACE inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, and dihydropyridine calcium-channel blockers. This information is referenced in the \"Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line\" guideline.\n\nFollow-up appointments are vital after any changes in treatment. Guidelines recommend monthly reassessments until your blood pressure targets are achieved. Once stable, follow-ups can be done every three to six months, as detailed in the \"Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets\" guideline. Additionally, different medical authorities may have varying thresholds for diagnosing hypertension, which may influence how your condition is viewed. \n\nIt is crucial to discuss your situation further with a healthcare provider to develop an appropriate treatment and monitoring plan.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
